# Product Copy Voice

The **default product voice** is **plain, clear, professional**. All
user-facing UI strings (labels, buttons, headings, empty states, loading
states, errors, tooltips, emails, toasts, microcopy) are short, scannable, and
honest — no marketing fluff, no hype, no exclamation spam. Plain voice is the
default for every UI string except the one place noted below.

This applies to user-visible strings only. **Code, comments, variable names,
commit messages, docs, and identifiers stay normal/professional.** Do not
touch field names, API contracts, or anything machine-facing.

## Grug voice — one place only

**Grug voice is confined to the expandable "grug explains" section per gem**
(the `GrugExplainer` component in `web/`). That section explains, in grug
cadence: what the app does, what problem it solves, and why grug thinks it's
working. The voice rules below apply to that section's copy and to its toggle
label — nowhere else.

- Tiny words. Short sentence. Sometimes drop article ("a"/"the") and pronoun.
- Lowercase by default. grug see, grug understand, grug click.
- Grug may refer to self as "grug" (the tool's voice), the user as "you".
- Plain truth over hype. No "leverage", "synergy", "seamless", "empower",
  "unlock", "supercharge", "delightful", "robust". No exclamation spam.
- Keep it usable: a reader must still understand the gem. Grug is simple, not
  cryptic. If a grug phrase is confusing, make it clearer, not cuter.

## Examples (grug-explains section only)

```
what it does → "app help you split bill with friend. snap photo, grug do math."
problem      → "before, friends argue who owe what. now no argue."
why working  → "ads run 40 day straight. spend going up. grug think real."
toggle label → "grug explain"
```

Everywhere else, use plain professional copy ("Loading…", "No results — adjust
filters.", "Couldn't sign in. Try again.", "Submit", "Reset layout").

## Keep as-is (product nouns)

Brand and metric names stay: **Field Desk**, **GemScore**, **cohort**. Don't
grug-ify proper nouns or numbers/units.
